1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,601
Put 100 units of electricity in.

2
00:00:02,833 --> 00:00:04,598
Get about 28 back.

3
00:00:05,364 --> 00:00:10,263
That's what engineers at the National Renewable Energy Laboratory

4
00:00:10,263 --> 00:00:15,161
in Colorado, now the National Laboratory of the Rockies,

5
00:00:15,161 --> 00:00:20,060
reported in 2024 after a megawatt-scale test: electricity into

6
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:23,870
hydrogen, into storage, and back into electricity.

7
00:00:24,252 --> 00:00:28,095
That was one research test system, running at full power.

8
00:00:28,606 --> 00:00:31,961
At low power, about 35 came back.

9
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,181
Now compare that with a whole fleet of batteries.

10
00:00:35,622 --> 00:00:38,937
In 2019, the EIA found that America's

11
00:00:38,937 --> 00:00:42,252
grid batteries gave back about 82 percent.

12
00:00:42,577 --> 00:00:45,317
That's called round-trip efficiency.

13
00:00:45,693 --> 00:00:47,742
So why did the federal government

14
00:00:47,742 --> 00:00:49,791
commit billions of dollars to hydrogen?

15
00:00:50,174 --> 00:00:52,717
And why might the hydrogen that matters

16
00:00:52,717 --> 00:00:55,260
most already be on your dinner table?

17
00:00:55,656 --> 00:00:59,805
Both answers come down to one idea: hydrogen isn't

18
00:00:59,805 --> 00:01:03,493
really a fuel problem, it's a where-do-you-use-it problem.

19
00:01:03,934 --> 00:01:07,124
So let's follow the electron, and see where turning

20
00:01:07,124 --> 00:01:09,959
it into a molecule is worth the trip.

21
00:01:10,798 --> 00:01:13,909
First, what hydrogen actually is.

22
00:01:14,293 --> 00:01:18,623
It's the most abundant element in the universe, but on

23
00:01:18,623 --> 00:01:22,953
Earth it comes combined with other elements: with oxygen in

24
00:01:22,953 --> 00:01:27,284
water, or with carbon in natural gas, coal and oil.

25
00:01:27,683 --> 00:01:29,268
So before we can use it,

26
00:01:29,268 --> 00:01:30,853
we have to pull it apart.

27
00:01:31,363 --> 00:01:35,684
The EIA calls hydrogen an energy carrier: it moves energy

28
00:01:35,684 --> 00:01:39,572
from one place to another, a bit like electricity.

29
00:01:39,971 --> 00:01:41,782
And carrying has a price.

30
00:01:42,107 --> 00:01:46,310
In the EIA's words, producing hydrogen requires more energy

31
00:01:46,310 --> 00:01:50,513
than hydrogen provides when it's converted to useful energy.

32
00:01:51,351 --> 00:01:56,532
America already makes plenty of it: about 10 million metric tons

33
00:01:56,532 --> 00:02:01,243
a year, according to the Energy Department's 2023 hydrogen strategy.

34
00:02:01,627 --> 00:02:05,790
The Energy Department says about 95 percent of U.S.

35
00:02:05,790 --> 00:02:09,953
hydrogen is made from natural gas, in large plants

36
00:02:09,953 --> 00:02:13,191
that use a process called steam-methane reforming.

37
00:02:13,576 --> 00:02:18,777
Methane and steam meet at 1,300 to 1,800 degrees Fahrenheit.

38
00:02:19,102 --> 00:02:24,117
Out come hydrogen and carbon monoxide, and a second step

39
00:02:24,117 --> 00:02:29,133
turns that carbon monoxide into carbon dioxide and more hydrogen.

40
00:02:29,521 --> 00:02:32,006
That carbon dioxide adds up.

41
00:02:32,818 --> 00:02:38,826
The Energy Department's 2023 hydrogen strategy put U.S. hydrogen production at

42
00:02:38,826 --> 00:02:44,289
about 100 million metric tons of greenhouse gases a year.

43
00:02:44,682 --> 00:02:48,210
Capture most of that carbon and store it underground,

44
00:02:48,210 --> 00:02:51,346
and people often call the result blue hydrogen.

45
00:02:52,043 --> 00:02:55,317
Let it go into the air, and it's called grey.

46
00:02:55,716 --> 00:02:59,853
The other main route is electrolysis: running an electric current

47
00:02:59,853 --> 00:03:03,576
through water to split it into hydrogen and oxygen.

48
00:03:04,017 --> 00:03:09,509
With wind or solar power, that's usually called green hydrogen.

49
00:03:09,885 --> 00:03:14,506
Either way, the IEA says low-emission hydrogen was still

50
00:03:14,506 --> 00:03:19,127
less than 1 percent of global production in 2024.

51
00:03:19,958 --> 00:03:22,164
Here's where the electron comes in.

52
00:03:22,547 --> 00:03:28,846
The Energy Department's 2022 status figure for a typical electrolyzer system

53
00:03:28,846 --> 00:03:35,144
was about 55 kilowatt-hours of electricity for every kilogram of hydrogen.

54
00:03:35,538 --> 00:03:38,585
Only about 61 percent of that electricity's

55
00:03:38,585 --> 00:03:41,633
energy ends up stored in the hydrogen.

56
00:03:42,446 --> 00:03:47,798
The department's long-term targets are around 70 percent.

57
00:03:48,191 --> 00:03:50,304
Then the hydrogen has to be stored.

58
00:03:50,536 --> 00:03:54,106
It holds more energy per pound than any other

59
00:03:54,106 --> 00:03:57,676
fuel, but very little per gallon, so it gets

60
00:03:57,676 --> 00:04:01,246
squeezed into tanks at up to 10,000 psi or

61
00:04:01,246 --> 00:04:04,816
chilled into a liquid at minus 423 degrees Fahrenheit.

62
00:04:05,658 --> 00:04:07,597
Now stack the losses.

63
00:04:08,038 --> 00:04:11,172
That's the chain from the start of this

64
00:04:11,172 --> 00:04:14,307
video: electricity to hydrogen, then back to electricity.

65
00:04:15,004 --> 00:04:19,712
In that Colorado test, the fuel cell generator alone turned

66
00:04:19,712 --> 00:04:24,420
only about half of the hydrogen's energy back into electricity.

67
00:04:24,816 --> 00:04:27,800
A hydrogen car runs the same chain.

68
00:04:28,612 --> 00:04:31,439
Its fuel cell turns hydrogen back

69
00:04:31,439 --> 00:04:34,267
into electricity for an electric motor.

70
00:04:35,219 --> 00:04:41,024
A battery car skips the detour and stores the electricity directly.

71
00:04:41,414 --> 00:04:43,272
Now look at the fueling map.

72
00:04:43,759 --> 00:04:47,494
As of November 2026, the Energy Department's station

73
00:04:47,494 --> 00:04:51,229
locator lists 44 hydrogen fueling stations in the

74
00:04:51,229 --> 00:04:54,498
whole country, 42 of them in California.

75
00:04:55,160 --> 00:05:00,408
It lists nearly 84,000 charging locations for electric cars.

76
00:05:00,807 --> 00:05:04,383
Home heating has the same problem, only sharper.

77
00:05:04,987 --> 00:05:08,818
The Energy Department says a heat pump can deliver up

78
00:05:08,818 --> 00:05:12,649
to three times more heat than the energy it uses.

79
00:05:13,043 --> 00:05:16,874
Turn that same electricity into hydrogen, and nearly 40 percent

80
00:05:16,874 --> 00:05:20,706
of the energy is gone before you light a burner.

81
00:05:21,309 --> 00:05:24,810
And hydrogen packs only about a third

82
00:05:24,810 --> 00:05:27,811
of natural gas's energy by volume.

83
00:05:28,204 --> 00:05:32,152
So for cars and homes, where electricity can do

84
00:05:32,152 --> 00:05:36,099
the job directly, hydrogen is the long way around.

85
00:05:36,946 --> 00:05:38,537
But flip the question.

86
00:05:38,978 --> 00:05:43,401
What if you don't need energy, but the hydrogen atoms themselves?

87
00:05:44,005 --> 00:05:46,805
That's where it makes sense, not as

88
00:05:46,805 --> 00:05:49,206
a fuel, but as an ingredient.

89
00:05:49,599 --> 00:05:51,515
Oil refineries are one.

90
00:05:51,724 --> 00:05:54,649
They use hydrogen to remove sulfur from fuels.

91
00:05:55,253 --> 00:05:59,260
Worldwide, refining used more than 43 million

92
00:05:59,260 --> 00:06:02,695
metric tons of hydrogen in 2024.

93
00:06:03,089 --> 00:06:04,877
Fertilizer is the big one.

94
00:06:05,202 --> 00:06:09,503
Hydrogen plus nitrogen from the air makes ammonia, and in

95
00:06:09,503 --> 00:06:13,375
the U.S., 88 percent of ammonia goes into fertilizer.

96
00:06:13,886 --> 00:06:17,950
So in a sense, that's the hydrogen on your dinner table.

97
00:06:18,329 --> 00:06:21,115
Worldwide, ammonia takes about 60 percent

98
00:06:21,115 --> 00:06:23,902
of the hydrogen used in industry.

99
00:06:24,598 --> 00:06:28,963
Switch to clean hydrogen, and the Energy Department says ammonia

100
00:06:28,963 --> 00:06:33,329
plants could cut their emissions by more than 90 percent.

101
00:06:33,725 --> 00:06:35,188
Steel is next.

102
00:06:35,466 --> 00:06:38,940
About 70 percent of American steel is made

103
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:41,980
by melting mostly scrap in electric furnaces.

104
00:06:42,583 --> 00:06:45,990
But making new steel from iron ore

105
00:06:45,990 --> 00:06:48,911
relies on coke and natural gas.

106
00:06:49,304 --> 00:06:53,097
Use clean hydrogen to pull the oxygen out of the

107
00:06:53,097 --> 00:06:56,890
ore instead, and the Energy Department says the life-cycle emissions

108
00:06:56,890 --> 00:07:00,682
of new steel could drop by 40 to 70 percent.

109
00:07:01,070 --> 00:07:02,614
Then come the maybes.

110
00:07:03,055 --> 00:07:05,777
Ships could run on fuels made

111
00:07:05,777 --> 00:07:08,500
from hydrogen, like ammonia and methanol.

112
00:07:08,891 --> 00:07:11,050
Heavy trucks are another maybe.

113
00:07:11,329 --> 00:07:15,637
The Energy Department says fuel cells suit trucks that

114
00:07:15,637 --> 00:07:19,944
need diesel-like fill times or ranges above 500 miles.

115
00:07:20,343 --> 00:07:24,512
And the grid could store hydrogen for days or even weeks,

116
00:07:24,512 --> 00:07:28,681
the EIA says, while the average U.S. grid battery in 2019

117
00:07:28,681 --> 00:07:32,092
held about an hour and a half of output.

118
00:07:32,789 --> 00:07:38,223
In Utah, a project backed by a federal loan guarantee

119
00:07:38,223 --> 00:07:43,656
is designed to pair a 220-megawatt electrolyzer with salt caverns.

120
00:07:44,497 --> 00:07:46,529
Which brings us back to the billions.

121
00:07:46,784 --> 00:07:50,541
The 2021 infrastructure law gave the Energy

122
00:07:50,541 --> 00:07:53,762
Department 9.5 billion dollars for hydrogen.

123
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:58,620
In 2023, the Energy Department selected seven regional

124
00:07:58,620 --> 00:08:02,841
hydrogen hubs, with 7 billion dollars in funding.

125
00:08:03,237 --> 00:08:07,550
Its national strategy aims for 10 million metric tons

126
00:08:07,550 --> 00:08:11,863
of clean hydrogen a year by 2030, 20 million

127
00:08:11,863 --> 00:08:15,218
by 2040, and 50 million by 2050.

128
00:08:15,603 --> 00:08:17,751
But plans have met reality.

129
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:21,924
In October 2025, the Energy Department cancelled funding

130
00:08:21,924 --> 00:08:25,077
for the California and Pacific Northwest hubs.

131
00:08:25,437 --> 00:08:28,671
In April 2026, it said it would keep

132
00:08:28,671 --> 00:08:31,904
nearly 5 billion dollars for the other five.

133
00:08:32,730 --> 00:08:33,856
Quick recap.

134
00:08:34,135 --> 00:08:36,852
Hydrogen is a carrier, not a source.

135
00:08:37,293 --> 00:08:41,722
America makes most of it from natural gas, and turning

136
00:08:41,722 --> 00:08:46,151
electricity into hydrogen and back loses most of the energy.

137
00:08:46,533 --> 00:08:48,768
That's why it struggles in cars

138
00:08:48,768 --> 00:08:51,003
and homes, where electricity works directly.

139
00:08:51,607 --> 00:08:54,875
But in refineries, fertilizer and steel,

140
00:08:54,875 --> 00:08:58,143
the molecule itself is the point.

141
00:08:58,533 --> 00:09:03,223
Hydrogen isn't a fuel problem, it's a where-do-you-use-it problem.

142
00:09:03,607 --> 00:09:06,422
So where would you use it: trucks, ships,

143
00:09:06,422 --> 00:09:09,237
steel, or storing power for a windless week?

144
00:09:09,679 --> 00:09:11,675
Tell me in the comments.

145
00:09:12,075 --> 00:09:15,425
If you like seeing the hidden machinery behind everyday

146
00:09:15,425 --> 00:09:18,402
life, subscribe to Other End of the Wire.

147
00:09:19,099 --> 00:09:21,015
Every source behind the numbers in

148
00:09:21,015 --> 00:09:22,930
this video is in the description.

149
00:09:23,174 --> 00:09:24,753
Follow the electron.
